//
// A generic body is typed once; a set call inside it is decided again in each instance
// (@PLN165 B3b).  `relay<U>`'s body is typed with `keep<T>`'s `-> T`, so at `integer` the call
// may not reach `keep(v: integer) -> Pt` — the instance would hand a record up where its
// body expects an integer.  Refused naming the definition it reaches, rather than answered.
//
// @falsified-at: 142e35694 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-set-member-reached-in-an-instance-returns-what-the-body-was-typed-with.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
//   CHANNEL: expectations — refused on both trees; only the refusal moves.
//   ARMED: nothing.
//   WITNESS: on the control the set call inside `relay` is refused in the template body,
//     "generic type U: method call requires a concrete type".
//   HOLDS: the program is refused on both trees.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: inside `relay`, the call `keep` reaches keep(integer) at (integer), which returns Pt — the generic body was typed with integer

struct Pt { x: integer, y: integer }
fn keep<T>(v: T) -> T { v }
fn keep(v: integer) -> Pt { Pt { x: 0, y: 0 } }
fn relay<U>(v: U) -> U { keep(v) }

fn main() {
  println("{relay(3)}");
}
